Z||000003||Ozzy||04-30-2000||12:51 PM||||I have not confirmed that this is the problem and am still not sure what to do about it but in your case, I'd be haulin' back to the alignment shop and making them check the car over real good at no cost! <P>I am yet to have a 4 wheel alignment. How much does it cost? I've heard as much as $100! If thats the case, and even if its less dollars, take it back to 'em. Take mine too if you like! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>If you do resolve this please let me know Sloponti. As I said earlier, I'm still not sure if mine is this toe problem or a wheel bearing yet.  Check out the thread by me "Whining noise in rear". There was some limited info there too.<P><BR>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||04-30-2000||02:06 PM||||Are you sure it's not the tires??  Are they the same brand and type all around?  Is there any vibration?||||reg||1
Z||000005||SloPonti||04-30-2000||05:31 PM||||the alignment was done at penske auto center for 50 bucks for a four wheel alighnment! this was also done by a friend of mine that works there! i am really good friends with him and he told me he would make sure that it got done right and like i said the car drives perfectly straight! i don't think it is the tires because it did not make this noise before the alignment and i did swap the front to back to see if this changed it but no change! they are all the same tires all the way around. i also checked the air pressure today in the tires and tried driving on different air pressures and no change! one thing i did notice though is that when i take corners at the 40 to 50mph range the noise goes away! any more thoughts on this would help!<BR>thanks<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-30-2000||09:14 PM||||Tire Tunes are most commonly caused by one or both of the following things.<P>The tire tread design is pumping air. On some designs this can get really lowd. (Some aftermarked alloy wheels can also sing.)<P>Vehicle harmonics. There are certain frequencies of vibration that will make the some or all of car sing. (In my case I have one interior trim pannel that rattles only at a particulay engine load and rpm)<P>Those 2 things can work together to make really annoying noises. <P>Other things that can do this are bent half/drive shafts, wheel out of balance, and bad engine dampers.<P>The reason this would stop durring cornering is that you upset the airflow and ballance of the whole car.<P>I assume by new CV's you mean new half shafts. I wouldn't assume new shafts are good. If they get dropped in shipping then they can be bent. not so you'd see it but enough they can make all sort of noise.<P>If it comes down to tires.... I think it will you likely can't fix it anyway but getting diferant tires. The reason it didn't start before now is that with the alignment off the tires are side loaded enough to upset the vibration. Only thing left to play with is air pressure. None stock tires don't always like factory presure. (My Cooper made Sears tires like 33psi all around.) It's not uncommon to have non stock tires running 2-4 PSI above or below stock pressure.<P>Also significantly wider tires may require slightly diferant alighnment than stock to run right.<P>NOTE: GM changed the rear alignment spec for ALL 84-87 Fiero. (GM TSB 87-3-10 03/87)<P>The rear toe is to be 0(Zero) +-.1 (1 tenth) of a degree on each wheel<P>Previous spec was .15 +- .1 degree per wheel.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||SloPonti||05-01-2000||12:04 AM||||so what should i do-should i go back to the alignment shop or the tire shop? <BR>thanks<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||05-01-2000||01:12 AM||||I'd talk to the alignment shop first, it'd be nice if they would recheck everything again just to make sure it's still in specs. Most reputable shops offer a limited warranty.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Guinea Pig||05-01-2000||01:41 AM||||I too have had this experience after alignment.  Both times replacing the tires cured the noise.  The explanation I was given is that the tires had "taken a set" to the old alignment.  Now re-aligned they are scrubbing against the former misalignment "set".  The problem always seemed worse if the tires were also rotated.  Don't know if this makes sense, but replacing the tires eliminated the noise.||||reg||1
Z||000011||SloPonti||05-01-2000||10:57 AM||||yeah but these are brand new tires! also i have switched the rear and front tires and still have the same noise! so i do think it is the alignment! does anyone have the current alignment specs for the 84 fiero||||reg||1
Z||000012||triker||05-01-2000||04:50 PM||||It's pretty easy to check your own alignment.  With the car sitting on level ground and without having been on jacks (when you let it down it will have a lot of positive camber), take a 2' level and place it horizontaly against the side walls of a rear tire.  sight along it from the rear to the front.  It should be pointing just inside of the outside of the front tire for a little of toe in.  To check camber I cut a piece of 1"x4" just long enough to fit between the beads on the wheel rim.  You may have to cut a notch out to go over the center of the rim where it sticks out further than the rim.  Place the piece of wood vertically against the rim and the level against it.  It should be about a quarter of a bubble in at the top for a little negative camber.  It's not exact but can tell you if you need to make a trip to the alignment shop with the family jewels in hand.||||reg||1
